        "id": 8324
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-07-31T13:07:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We stamped activeGenerations, then the initConnection() failed. We\u0027re returning a failedFuture, but we\u0027re not clearing the stamp. And since the node is in neither activeInitializers nor activeDevices, the close() loop will not reach it. Yes - a later connectDevice() overwrites the stale stamp — but during that window isCurrentGeneration() reports \"true\" for a dead attempt.",
      "revId": "b471a67ec693a5734b2e0f53f0d35da95ffa1323",
      "serverId": "7fc14799-209e-464c-9743-7a06c2c21a81"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2fa46837_cb1342a9",
        "filename": "modules/gnmi-sb/src/main/java/org/opendaylight/gnmi/southbound/device/connection/DeviceConnectionManager.java",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 168,
      "author": {
        "id": 8324
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-07-31T13:07:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We\u0027re returning Futures.immediateFuture(null) here in the failed case, as well as below in the successful case? Why? This means that prepareDeviceConnection() has no idea which one it\u0027s getting. And even if it receives this failed one it proceeds to call deviceConnection.setDeviceStatusReady() on this closed connection. Return failed/cancelled future instead and check it in the caller.",
      "revId": "b471a67ec693a5734b2e0f53f0d35da95ffa1323",
      "serverId": "7fc14799-209e-464c-9743-7a06c2c21a81"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ecc2a745_cf2c7637",
        "filename": "modules/gnmi-sb/src/main/java/org/opendaylight/gnmi/southbound/device/connection/DeviceConnectionManager.java",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 176,
      "author": {
        "id": 8324
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-07-31T13:07:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The fact that this call is outside the synchronized block introduces the same problem this entire patch tries to fix. If Thread-1 enters createMountPoint(), takes the lock, finished the synchronized block and starts executing logic in  saveCapabilitiesList(). During this the DTCL receives the config-delete (GnmiNodeListener.disconnectNode()) on Thread-2. Since the lock is free at this point, the Thread-2 takes it and simply deletes the node. If this deletion happens before Thread-1 reaches WriteTransaction tx \u003d dataBroker.newWriteOnlyTransaction(), Thread-1 will recreate the node again. Consider adding the config-presence check to saveCapabilitiesList() - this should shrink the race-window.",
      "revId": "b471a67ec693a5734b2e0f53f0d35da95ffa1323",
      "serverId": "7fc14799-209e-464c-9743-7a06c2c21a81"
    },
